<body>
\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>Traffic on a network fundamentally flows in one of three ways depending on the destination intent.</p>
            
            <h3>1. Unicast</h3>
            <p><strong>Unicast</strong> is one-to-one communication. A single sender transmits data to a single specific destination.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Example:</em> Your PC loading a web page from a specific Web Server. Most internet traffic is unicast.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>2. Broadcast</h3>
            <p><strong>Broadcast</strong> is one-to-all communication. A single sender transmits data to <em>every</em> device within the local broadcast domain (typically synonymous with a <a href="what-is-vlan.html">VLAN</a>).</p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Example:</em> An <a href="what-is-arp.html">ARP</a> request where a PC asks the entire network, "Who has IP 192.168.1.1?"</li>
                <li><strong>Important Distinction:</strong> <a href="ipv6-fundamentals.html">IPv6</a> does NOT use broadcast traffic. IPv6 relies entirely on targeted multicast to achieve similar goals.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>3. Multicast</h3>
            <p><strong>Multicast</strong> is one-to-many communication. A single sender transmits one copy of the data, and the network hardware replicates it only to the specific group of devices that have explicitly requested to receive it.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Example:</em> A live video stream being sent to hundreds of employees. Sending unicast would overwhelm the server; sending broadcast would overwhelm the entire network. Multicast efficiently sends one stream that is duplicated at the edge switches.</li>
                <li><em>Management:</em> IPv4 relies conceptually on IGMP (Internet Group Management Protocol) for clients to join a multicast group. IPv6 relies on MLD (Multicast Listener Discovery).</li>
            </ul>
        
        </div>
        
